{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>I will greatly multiply.</b> It should have said 'the sorrow of your pregnancy.'" "<b>But</b> it is possible to explain that 'your sorrow' and 'your pregnancy' are two things. For the meaning of 'sorrow' (עצבון) is the suffering of the menstrual period, which is a natural malady in a woman, as they said: 'her head and limbs are heavy upon her'; and because this malady persists in coming upon her anew from month to month, therefore the expression is doubled: 'I will greatly multiply' (הרבה ארבה). And 'your pregnancy' (הריונך) is the suffering of pregnancy. 'In pain you shall bear children' is the suffering of childbirth." "<b>And to your husband shall be your desire.</b> For even though the woman is subjugated under the authority of the husband, and the way of the servant is to flee from his master so that he not be enslaved, He decreed in this that she shall long for her husband and that she shall desire to be subjugated to him — the opposite of the usual way." "<b>And he shall rule over you.</b> This punishment — that the husband shall be the leader and commander over her — in place of her having commanded him to eat of the fruit; and this punishment is measure for measure." "<b>And behold,</b> they are five curses upon the woman: the suffering of the menstrual period, the suffering of pregnancy, the suffering of childbirth, that she shall long for him, and that he shall command over her. And likewise they said further: there are five impure bloods in a woman, and this corresponds to five parts of desire in which she sinned in the eating of the fruit, and they are: the seeing of the heart, the desire of the eyes, the craving of the intellect, the eating of the fruit, and the giving to her husband. This is what is written: ״ותרא האשה כי טוב העץ למאכל וכי תאוה הוא לעינים ונחמד העץ להשכיל ותקח מפריו ותאכל ותתן גם לאישה עמה״ — 'And the woman saw that the tree was good for food and that it was a desire to the eyes and that the tree was desirable for understanding, and she took of its fruit and ate and gave also to her husband with her.'" "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> הרבה ארבה — everything that is in הרבה is in ארבה. Its explanation: הרב\"ה — days, and then she shall live. And there are in it more than 3 days, for seven months are 210, but they are 3 days of reception (קליטה), for any batch of seed that is not received within 3 days is not received, and the day of reception counts toward the reckoning."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {5 "The Hebrew text is obscure; הרב\"ה appears to be the word הרבה with an abbreviation mark, and the argument concerns the additional letters/meaning in ארבה. The subject of 'she shall live' (תחיה, here masculine יחיה in the source) and the 210/7-months/3-days calculation is translated hyper-literally without smoothing."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:09:53.972378737Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}